<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<solver xmlns="https://timefold.ai/xsd/solver"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="https://timefold.ai/xsd/solver https://timefold.ai/xsd/solver/solver.xsd">

    <!--
        Fixed seed: makes solves reproducible run-to-run, which is what let us
        diagnose the local-optimum problem below in the first place. Remove if
        solution diversity across repeated solves is ever wanted back.
    -->
    <!--
        Avoid the assert modes, which re-run a full score recalculation to check
        for score corruption at every phase boundary and keep the intrusive
        bookkeeping needed to do so. If score corruption is ever suspected, set
        this to PHASE_ASSERT (or FULL_ASSERT to pinpoint the guilty move).

        This was REPRODUCIBLE on Timefold 1.x. That mode is gone in 2.x, where
        reproducibility is no longer a mode but a consequence of the fixed
        randomSeed below: NO_ASSERT is the same trade-off under the new naming
        (no checks, same seed, same plan). The whole tuning recorded further
        down rests on that determinism, so a run that stops being reproducible
        invalidates those measurements rather than merely being noisy.

        Element order is imposed by the XSD (SolverConfig's propOrder):
        environmentMode, then randomSeed, then moveThreadCount.
    -->
    <environmentMode>NO_ASSERT</environmentMode>

    <randomSeed>0</randomSeed>

    <!--
        No <moveThreadCount>: multi-threaded incremental solving requires
        Timefold Solver Enterprise Edition (commercial). Measured here — the
        solver refuses to start with "Enterprise Edition could not be loaded".
        The 8 cores of the host stay unusable by the solver as a result.
    -->


    <solutionClass>dev.sylvain.planning.domain.PlanningEvenement</solutionClass>
    <entityClass>dev.sylvain.planning.domain.PosteAffectation</entityClass>

    <scoreDirectorFactory>
        <constraintProviderClass>dev.sylvain.planning.solver.PlanningConstraintProvider</constraintProviderClass>
    </scoreDirectorFactory>

    <!--
        Explicit pipeline. FIRST_FIT_DECREASING builds the initial assignment,
        then Late Acceptance does the heavy lifting. A dedicated union move
        selector (change + swap) keeps a healthy move diversity so the search
        can trade an "unavailable" assignment for an available animateur
        rather than stalling. Termination (seconds / unimproved-seconds) is
        injected programmatically by PlanningService.

        FIRST_FIT_DECREASING (not FIRST_FIT): postes are processed hardest
        first, via PosteAffectationDifficultyComparatorFactory which ranks
        them by how many animateurs are actually eligible (competence +
        availability). Plain FIRST_FIT processed postes in declaration order,
        so easy postes greedily consumed animateurs that scarce postes needed
        later, regularly leaving several postes unpourvus/double-booked
        (hard violations) for local search to repair from a bad starting
        point — a major source of the run-to-run inconsistency this fixes.
        Under maxJoursConsecutifsTravaillesDur the same comparator puts the
        calendar first, earliest day first, then scarcity within a day
        (ADR 0068).

        The animateurRange value range spans every animateur (~150): eligibility
        depends on the target poste, not on a static animateur property, so it
        cannot be narrowed at the @ValueRangeProvider level. EligibleAnimateurMoveFilter
        rejects moves that assign an incompetent or unavailable animateur before
        the solver pays for a score calculation, which is what keeps the full
        ~150 animateurs / 2000+ postes scenario solvable in reasonable time on
        constrained hardware (e.g. a Raspberry Pi).
    -->
    <!--
        On a very large problem — past 15 000 000 seats x animateurs — this phase
        is replaced at solve time by a sampled one that scores 50 eligible animateurs
        drawn at random per seat (LargeProblemConstruction, ADR 0036). Its cost
        follows seats x animateurs, a little more as the plan fills: 182 s for
        6 480 seats and 1 000 animateurs, 50 min for 41 370 seats, where the
        sampled one reaches zero hard in 3 min 30 s. Every real edition sits far
        under the threshold and keeps the phase below as written.
    -->
    <constructionHeuristic>
        <constructionHeuristicType>FIRST_FIT_DECREASING</constructionHeuristicType>
        <changeMoveSelector>
            <filterClass>dev.sylvain.planning.solver.EligibleAnimateurMoveFilter$ChangeMoveFilter</filterClass>
        </changeMoveSelector>
    </constructionHeuristic>
    <localSearch>
        <!-- Phase 1 : atteindre la faisabilite, puis passer la main. -->
        <termination>
            <bestScoreFeasible>true</bestScoreFeasible>
        </termination>
        <unionMoveSelector>
            <changeMoveSelector>
                <filterClass>dev.sylvain.planning.solver.EligibleAnimateurMoveFilter$ChangeMoveFilter</filterClass>
                <fixedProbabilityWeight>1.0</fixedProbabilityWeight>
            </changeMoveSelector>
            <swapMoveSelector>
                <filterClass>dev.sylvain.planning.solver.EligibleAnimateurMoveFilter$SwapMoveFilter</filterClass>
                <fixedProbabilityWeight>1.0</fixedProbabilityWeight>
            </swapMoveSelector>
            <!--
                Uniform Change/Swap selection over ~2000+ postes samples the
                handful still unassigned far too rarely to clear the last
                posteDoitEtrePourvu violations within the time budget (empirically:
                the solver plateaus at 1-10 unfilled seats out of 2088 even after
                the full 180s). These two selectors restrict one side of the pair
                to unassigned postes and get an equal share of move attempts
                (fixedProbabilityWeight), so filling an empty seat directly, or by
                displacing someone already scheduled elsewhere at a different
                slot, is tried constantly instead of by chance.
            -->
            <changeMoveSelector>
                <filterClass>dev.sylvain.planning.solver.EligibleAnimateurMoveFilter$ChangeMoveFilter</filterClass>
                <fixedProbabilityWeight>1.0</fixedProbabilityWeight>
                <entitySelector>
                    <filterClass>dev.sylvain.planning.solver.UnassignedPosteFilter</filterClass>
                </entitySelector>
            </changeMoveSelector>
            <swapMoveSelector>
                <filterClass>dev.sylvain.planning.solver.EligibleAnimateurMoveFilter$SwapMoveFilter</filterClass>
                <fixedProbabilityWeight>1.0</fixedProbabilityWeight>
                <entitySelector>
                    <filterClass>dev.sylvain.planning.solver.UnassignedPosteFilter</filterClass>
                </entitySelector>
            </swapMoveSelector>
            <!--
                Ruin and recreate around the holes (ADR 0025): a hole at a
                saturated hour is filled by a chain of moves whose links are
                hard-neutral and medium-worse once the published plan has a
                price, so change and swap never walk it. Ruining a handful of
                seats at the hole's hour and rebuilding them with the
                construction heuristic evaluates the chain as ONE move.
                HoleNeighbourPosteFilter keeps the ruined set at that hour.

                It selects nothing when the plan has no empty seat — which is
                NOT the same as "the phase is over": this phase ends on
                feasibility, and most hard rules are broken by OVER-assignment
                (weekly hours, rest, the ad hoc ones), so "every seat filled,
                still infeasible" is an ordinary state. In it a filter that
                accepts nothing makes Timefold try entityCount * 10 times
                before giving up, which is why the filter memoises the hours of
                the holes: 40 700 ns per call scanning, 69 ns from the memo, on
                3 503 seats.

                The recreate step runs its own construction heuristic, built by
                Timefold with no filterClass: EligibleAnimateurMoveFilter does
                NOT screen its candidates, so a ruin move scores 3 to 10 seats
                against every animateur. That is the whole reason phase 1 drops
                from ~6 800 to ~30 move evaluations per second here, and it is
                worth re-checking on a Timefold bump. Checked again on 2.7.0
                (timefold-solver-core-2.7.0-sources.jar, Maven Central): still
                no knob. RuinRecreateMoveSelectorConfig takes an entitySelector,
                a variableName and the ruined counts and percentages, nothing
                for the recreate side; RuinRecreateMoveSelectorFactory hands
                RuinRecreateConstructionHeuristicPhaseBuilder a bare
                QueuedEntityPlacerConfig with the default entity selector, whose
                values are never filtered. 2.6 to 2.7 only passes the enclosing
                phase's environment mode to that nested heuristic. The one lever
                Timefold would honour there is an entity-dependent value range
                on PosteAffectation — a model change, not a configuration one.
            -->
            <ruinRecreateMoveSelector>
                <fixedProbabilityWeight>1.0</fixedProbabilityWeight>
                <minimumRuinedCount>3</minimumRuinedCount>
                <maximumRuinedCount>10</maximumRuinedCount>
                <entitySelector>
                    <filterClass>dev.sylvain.planning.solver.HoleNeighbourPosteFilter</filterClass>
                </entitySelector>
            </ruinRecreateMoveSelector>
            <!--
                Relocation through the week (WeekRelocationMoveIteratorFactory):
                the chain the two selectors above cannot reach. The people free
                at an empty seat's hour are often the ones already at six days
                that week, so filling it means giving up one of their other
                days, whose seats then need colleagues. Ruin-and-recreate stays
                at the hole's hour and never touches Monday; a change move sees
                only the first link. This move plays the whole chain at once
                and leaves the verdict to the score. Measured on the 2026
                edition: 26 montage seats stayed empty for 1800 s without it,
                every one fillable by such a chain.
            -->
            <moveIteratorFactory>
                <fixedProbabilityWeight>1.0</fixedProbabilityWeight>
                <moveIteratorFactoryClass>dev.sylvain.planning.solver.WeekRelocationMoveIteratorFactory</moveIteratorFactoryClass>
            </moveIteratorFactory>
        </unionMoveSelector>
        <acceptor>
            <lateAcceptanceSize>400</lateAcceptanceSize>
        </acceptor>
        <forager>
            <!--
                Number of candidate moves sampled per local-search step. This
                was 4, then 20 for the quality of each step on the 2088-poste
                reference scenario. Measured again on the real 3502-poste 2026
                scenario (153 animateurs, 45 premium stands), same seed, same
                180 s budget, runs executed sequentially:

                    acceptedCountLimit :  1     2     3    4    10    20    40
                    score dur a 180 s  :  0     0    -1   -3    -6   -14   -27
                    0 hard atteint a   : 95s  137s     jamais dans le budget

                The trade-off inverted with the problem size: at 3502 postes a
                step costs enough that sampling 20 candidates starves the
                search, and the setting that was "safer" never reaches
                feasibility at all — the 1800 s production run stalled at
                -2 hard while 2 gets there in under three minutes.

                lateAcceptanceSize was re-measured alongside: 100 / 400 / 1500
                give -14 / -14 / -12 at acceptedCountLimit 20, and -1 / 0 / 0
                at 2. Below 400 it hurts, above it changes nothing: left as is.

                The table above is Timefold 1.x and does NOT transfer: on 2.5 a
                local-search step evaluates ~278 moves where 1.34 evaluated ~24,
                so the effective sampling is ten times wider without the value
                having changed. Re-measured on the production profile (3499
                postes, 153 animateurs), same seed, 1800 s, runs sequential:

                    acceptedCountLimit :      1          2
                    score dur a 180 s  :     -2        -10
                    0 hard atteint a   :    333s       526s
                    medium a 1800 s    : -4 697     -4 748

                Hence 1, where 1.x wanted 2, and the reason is that the 1.x
                argument no longer holds: back then 1 reached feasibility
                earlier but polished visibly worse, so 2 bought quality. On 2.5
                a step already samples ten times wider, so lowering the count
                no longer starves anything — 1 is feasible 1.6x sooner AND ends
                with the better medium. It gives up only soft (-368 vs -336),
                the level that yields to the other two by construction.

                Measured on the production profile rather than on
                festival-realiste, which understates it by a third (0 hard at
                359 s, construction heuristic ending at -110 hard instead of
                -218) — and never on its canicule variant, which reaches
                feasibility in the construction heuristic and so does not
                exercise this setting at all.
            -->
            <acceptedCountLimit>1</acceptedCountLimit>
        </forager>
    </localSearch>

    <!--
        Seconde phase : le planning est faisable, il reste a le rendre bon.
        Les deux objectifs ne demandent pas le meme reglage — mesure sur le
        scenario 2026 (3502 postes), budget de 600 s, meme graine :

            configuration      0 hard atteint   medium   soft
            acl 2 seul             146 s        -7 987   -539
            acl 20 seul            jamais       -6 878   -720   (-2 hard)
            2 puis 20              145 s        -6 876   -715
            2 puis 40              145 s        -6 704   -716

        Des pas rapides trouvent la faisabilite et des pas larges la polissent :
        enchainer les deux donne 0 hard ET le meilleur medium, la ou chaque
        reglage seul echoue sur l'un des deux. Timefold conserve toujours la
        meilleure solution rencontrée, donc la phase 2 ne peut pas reperdre la
        faisabilite acquise par la phase 1.

        40 plutot que 20 en phase 2 : verifie sur trois graines, meme sens et
        meme ordre de grandeur a chaque fois.

            graine      0        1        2
            2 puis 20   -6 876   -6 867   -6 879
            2 puis 40   -6 704   -6 703   -6 762

        Le soft est marginalement moins bon (-716/-745/-723 contre
        -715/-725/-713), mais il est classe sous le medium : l'arbitrage est
        sans ambiguite.

        Les deux selecteurs cibles sur les postes non pourvus ne sont pas
        repris ici : arrive a ce stade il n'y en a plus, ils ne produiraient
        aucun mouvement.

        Mouvements pillar (un bloc d'un animateur sur une journee) : mesures
        et ecartes, Timefold 2.7.0. Protocole : phase 1 jouee une fois par
        scenario (graine 0), puis cette phase seule depuis ce meme plan
        faisable, 90 s, graines 0/1/2, runs sequentiels. Variante essayee :
        pillarChangeMoveSelector, sous-piliers SEQUENCE de 2 a 3 postes tries
        par date puis heure, filtre n'acceptant qu'un bloc d'une seule date
        dont chaque poste est eligible pour l'animateur qui le recoit, poids
        0.5 a cote du change et du swap. Medium atteint, graines 0/1/2 :

            scenario     sans pillar                avec pillar
            complet      -30 805/-30 945/-30 770    -31 165/-31 185/-31 155
            continu      -31 685/-31 695/-31 650    -31 885/-31 940/-31 960
            canicule     -22 215/-22 275/-22 310    -22 700/-22 835/-22 870
            hivernal     -33 770/-33 815/-33 840    -33 975/-34 085/-33 985
            gamme-20     -38 020/-38 115/-38 135    -37 860/-37 705/-37 915
            gamme-22     -34 570/-34 675/-34 710    -35 280/-35 215/-35 150
            gamme-23     -39 445/-39 475/-39 505    -37 415/-37 620/-37 670
            gamme-24     -43 740/-43 655/-43 645    -44 080/-43 925/-44 005
            gamme-25     -26 315/-26 375/-26 365    -26 420/-26 485/-26 515

        Sept scenarios sur neuf perdent du medium a chaque graine, dont les
        deux fixtures reelles ; gamme-20 en gagne 160 a 410 mais perd 1 700 a
        4 700 de soft ; seul gamme-23 (souhaits, ad hoc) gagne nettement,
        +1 800 a +2 000. Ailleurs le soft ne bouge dans aucun sens constant.
        Vitesse : 0,3 a 6 % d'evaluations par seconde en moins (3 470 contre
        3 310/s sur complet, 4 880 contre 4 690/s sur canicule). Un animateur
        tient le plus souvent un seul poste par jour (canicule : 1 382
        journees a un poste, 306 a deux), si bien que le filtre ecarte 66 a
        92 % des blocs tires. Le pillarSwapMoveSelector, blocs a la meme date,
        est pire : sur canicule il n'accepte que 0,03 % des paires tirees et
        divise la vitesse par neuf (4 620 contre 535/s, 60 s, graine 0).
        Aucun gain net et constant : non retenu.

        L'echange de journees (DaySwapMoveIteratorFactory) fait ce que le
        pillar swap ne pouvait pas : il choisit d'abord un bloc (animateur,
        date) parmi ceux qui existent, puis un collegue du meme jour eligible
        dans les deux sens, au lieu de tirer deux blocs au hasard et d'en
        filtrer 99,97 %. Il garde le nombre de jours de chacun, sa semaine et
        sa serie de jours : seul ce que chacun fait ce jour-la change. Meme
        protocole que ci-dessus, medium atteint, graines 0/1/2 :

            scenario     sans echange               avec echange (poids 2)
            complet      -30 700/-30 875/-30 685    -23 795/-23 790/-23 950
            continu      -31 645/-31 665/-31 570    -29 715/-29 580/-29 680
            canicule     -21 965/-21 975/-22 120    -19 165/-19 215/-19 210
            hivernal     -33 685/-33 770/-33 795    -30 010/-30 120/-30 180
            gamme-20     -37 940/-37 855/-37 860    -33 525/-33 810/-33 325
            gamme-22     -34 315/-34 330/-34 420    -32 275/-32 125/-32 135
            gamme-23     -38 555/-38 180/-37 145    -32 475/-31 325/-31 250
            gamme-24     -43 350/-43 015/-43 150    -41 545/-41 400/-41 455
            gamme-25     -26 150/-26 220/-26 190    -25 005/-25 100/-25 145

        Gain a chaque graine de chaque scenario, de +1 045 (gamme-25) a
        +7 085 (complet). Le soft progresse sur canicule et continu, recule
        sur gamme-20 (-4 700 a -6 250), gamme-23 et gamme-24, varie ailleurs :
        il est classe sous le medium. Prix : 11 a 31 % d'evaluations par
        seconde en moins (5 470 contre 4 850/s sur canicule), et la moitie
        des tirages seulement pour change et swap. Sur canicule, 1 086 des 1 414 pas de la graine 0 sont des
        echanges. Poids : sur canicule et gamme-22 a 60 s (graine 0), 0,25 /
        0,5 / 1 / 2 / 4 donnent -21 650 / -20 795 / -20 470 / -19 965 /
        -19 870 et -34 455 / -34 085 / -33 610 / -33 350 / -33 200 ; 4 ne
        gagne plus que 95 et 150 sur 2, dans l'ecart entre graines, et ne
        laisse qu'un tiers des tirages a change et swap. La variante
        « donner sa journee a un collegue libre ce jour-la » ne gagnait rien
        (-23 135 contre -23 170, -35 320 contre -35 390) : non retenue.
    -->
    <localSearch>
        <unionMoveSelector>
            <changeMoveSelector>
                <filterClass>dev.sylvain.planning.solver.EligibleAnimateurMoveFilter$ChangeMoveFilter</filterClass>
                <fixedProbabilityWeight>1.0</fixedProbabilityWeight>
            </changeMoveSelector>
            <swapMoveSelector>
                <filterClass>dev.sylvain.planning.solver.EligibleAnimateurMoveFilter$SwapMoveFilter</filterClass>
                <fixedProbabilityWeight>1.0</fixedProbabilityWeight>
            </swapMoveSelector>
            <!--
                Echange de journees (DaySwapMoveIteratorFactory) : les postes
                d'un animateur a une date passent a un collegue qui travaille
                ce jour-la, et les siens a lui. Poids 2 : mesure ci-dessus.
            -->
            <moveIteratorFactory>
                <fixedProbabilityWeight>2.0</fixedProbabilityWeight>
                <moveIteratorFactoryClass>dev.sylvain.planning.solver.DaySwapMoveIteratorFactory</moveIteratorFactoryClass>
            </moveIteratorFactory>
        </unionMoveSelector>
        <acceptor>
            <lateAcceptanceSize>400</lateAcceptanceSize>
        </acceptor>
        <forager>
            <acceptedCountLimit>40</acceptedCountLimit>
        </forager>
    </localSearch>

</solver>
